IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `lpact-dates`

-- Legal Profession Act 2006 (ACT), Part 3.2 (costs disclosure and assessment) -- the questions of the client.
--
-- SEAT: the client: the person who retains a lawyer (a "law practice") for a matter -- a personal injury or workers
-- compensation claim, a will dispute, a discrimination complaint -- and pays, or is asked to pay, the legal costs. "Does
-- this Part apply to my matter?", "Must my lawyer tell me what it will cost?", "Is my no-win no-fee agreement valid?",
-- "Can I still cool off?", "Is the uplift fee allowed?", "What can my lawyer recover if the agreement is void?", "Can I
-- ask for an itemised bill?", "When can my lawyer sue me?", "Am I in time to have the bill assessed?". The Supreme Court,
-- the law society council and the ACAT get no seat: what they would decide is asked as a fact ("if the court is
-- satisfied that ...").
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, a number, a date, a choice from a list). No question takes a DEONTIC or an
-- EVENT. Two duties with deadlines are regulative rules run inside questions: the law practice's bar on suing before the
-- days set by s 289 (1) and s 292 (6), (7) have passed, and the client's 12 months to apply for a costs assessment
-- (s 294A (5)).
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: s 261 (definitions used), ss 263 to 265 (application), s 269 with ss 271, 272 (duty to disclose
-- and its exceptions), s 274 (uplift disclosure), s 277 (effect of failure to disclose), s 278 (progress reports), s 279
-- (basis of recovery), s 281 (interest), Division 3.2.5 whole (ss 282 to 288: costs agreements, conditional costs
-- agreements, uplift fees, contingency fees, void agreements, setting aside), ss 289, 292 (billing and suing),
-- s 294A (5) to (7) (time to apply for assessment), s 298 (b), s 300C, s 302, ss 304, 304A, s 307A. Out (NOTES.md).
--
-- Edition: Legal Profession Act 2006 (ACT), A2006-25, Republication No 45 (R45), effective 16 May 2026, deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/A2006-25.txt. The interest rate of s 281 (4) (b) is read from the Court Procedures Rules
-- 2006 (SL2006-29), schedule 2, part 2.2, deposited at ../../../court-procedures-rules-2006/registers/source-bundle/.
--
-- (c) Australian Capital Territory. Used under CC BY 4.0 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md). Unofficial text.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Judgments the Act hands to the Supreme Court or to the law practice
-- are INPUTS. Answers follow the rulings on negatives of 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05 (NOTES.md): an answer that is the
-- text's own no is marked "(the text's no)"; an answer where the text is limited by its own wording, or silent, is
-- marked "NOTHING-kind" or is NOTHING.

§ `Legal Profession Act 2006 (ACT), Part 3.2: the questions of the client`

-- s 292 (10) and s 269 (6): "threshold amount means (a) $1 500 (exclusive of GST and disbursements); or (b) if a higher
-- amount is prescribed by regulation -- the higher amount." s 272 (1) (a) uses the same $1 500 (exclusive of GST,
-- excluding disbursements). No regulation is deposited; the Act's own figure is used (NOTES.md).
`the threshold amount` MEANS 1500

-- s 284 (4) (b): the uplift fee must not exceed 25% of the legal costs (excluding disbursements) otherwise payable.
`the uplift cap` MEANS 25%

-- s 302 (2) (a): reduced by 15% or more.
`the assessment reduction` MEANS 15%

-- Court Procedures Rules 2006, schedule 2, items 2.3 and 2.4: interest on judgment after 30 June 2010 is 6% above the
-- cash rate last published by the Reserve Bank of Australia before the start of the half-year.
`the margin over the cash rate` MEANS 6

-- s 261 "sophisticated client": a client to whom disclosure is not required because of s 272 (1) (c) (the listed kinds
-- of client) or (d) (costs agreed after a tender process).
GIVEN `listed in s 272 (1) (c)` IS A BOOLEAN, `agreed after a tender` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a sophisticated client` `listed in s 272 (1) (c)` `agreed after a tender` IF
        `listed in s 272 (1) (c)`
    OR  `agreed after a tender`

§§ `ss 263 to 265 -- Does Part 3.2 apply to my matter`

-- s 263: "This part applies to a matter if the client first instructs the law practice in relation to the matter in the
-- ACT." s 266: where the practice first receives the instructions, by any means of communication.
DECLARE `Where I first instructed the law practice` IS ONE OF
    `in the ACT`
    `in another Australian jurisdiction`
    `it is not possible to decide where`

DECLARE `Whether Part 3.2 applies to my matter` IS ONE OF
    `Part 3.2 applies: you first instructed the law practice in the ACT (s 263)`
    `Part 3.2 applies: you accepted the law practice's written offer that it is to apply (s 264 (2) (a))`
    `Part 3.2 applies: you notified the law practice in writing that you require it to apply (s 264 (2) (b))`
    `Part 3.2 has ceased to apply: the matter moved under another jurisdiction's corresponding law (s 265 (2)); costs incurred while it applied stay under it (s 268 (2))`
    `Part 3.2 does not apply: your notification came more than 28 days after you were told of your right to give it, and has no effect (s 264 (4), the text's no)`
    `Part 3.2 does not apply on these facts (the text's no: neither s 263 nor s 264 is met)`

-- s 264 (4): "A notification has no effect ... if it is given after the period of 28 days after the day the law
-- practice discloses to the client (under a corresponding law) information about the client's right to make a
-- notification of that kind". Day 28 after the disclosure is the last day it has effect.
GIVEN `notified` IS A BOOLEAN, `notified on` IS A DATE, `told of the right` IS A BOOLEAN, `told on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the notification is late` `notified` `notified on` `told of the right` `told on` IF
        `notified`
    AND `told of the right`
    AND `the days from` `told on` `to` `notified on` GREATER THAN 28

@export Does Part 3.2 of the Legal Profession Act 2006 (ACT) -- the rules on costs disclosure, costs agreements, bills and costs assessment -- apply to my matter with my lawyer (ss 263 to 265)?
GIVEN `where` IS A `Where I first instructed the law practice` @desc Where the law practice first received your instructions in the matter (in person, by post, phone, email or otherwise)
      `services in the ACT` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the legal services are or will be provided completely or mainly in the ACT
      `connected with the ACT` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the matter has a substantial connection with the ACT
      `accepted the offer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you accepted (in writing or by conduct) a written offer from the law practice that Part 3.2 of the ACT Act is to apply to the matter
      `offer stated it all` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that offer clearly stated that it was an offer to agree that the Part applies, that you could accept it in writing or by conduct, and what conduct would be acceptance
      `notified` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you notified the law practice in writing that you require Part 3.2 of the ACT Act to apply
      `notified on` IS A DATE @desc The day you gave that notification (any date if you did not)
      `told of the right` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the law practice told you, under another jurisdiction's law, of your right to give such a notification
      `told on` IS A DATE @desc The day it told you (any date if it did not)
      `elsewhere` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the legal services are or will be provided completely or mainly in another jurisdiction, or the matter has a substantial connection with another jurisdiction
      `chose the other law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you agreed with the law practice, or notified it in time, under the other jurisdiction's law that its corresponding provisions are to apply
GIVETH A `Whether Part 3.2 applies to my matter`
`ss 263 to 265 -- does Part 3.2 apply to my matter` `where` `services in the ACT` `connected with the ACT` `accepted the offer` `offer stated it all` `notified` `notified on` `told of the right` `told on` `elsewhere` `chose the other law` MEANS
    IF      `applies at all`
        AND `elsewhere`
        AND `chose the other law`
    THEN `Part 3.2 has ceased to apply: the matter moved under another jurisdiction's corresponding law (s 265 (2)); costs incurred while it applied stay under it (s 268 (2))`
    ELSE IF `first instructed here`
         THEN `Part 3.2 applies: you first instructed the law practice in the ACT (s 263)`
         ELSE IF `s 264 (1) (a), (b) met` AND `the offer took effect`
              THEN `Part 3.2 applies: you accepted the law practice's written offer that it is to apply (s 264 (2) (a))`
              ELSE IF `s 264 (1) (a), (b) met` AND `the notice took effect`
                   THEN `Part 3.2 applies: you notified the law practice in writing that you require it to apply (s 264 (2) (b))`
                   ELSE IF `s 264 (1) (a), (b) met` AND `the notification is late` `notified` `notified on` `told of the right` `told on`
                        THEN `Part 3.2 does not apply: your notification came more than 28 days after you were told of your right to give it, and has no effect (s 264 (4), the text's no)`
                        ELSE `Part 3.2 does not apply on these facts (the text's no: neither s 263 nor s 264 is met)`
    WHERE
        `first instructed here` MEANS `where` EQUALS `in the ACT`
        `s 264 (1) (a), (b) met` MEANS
                (NOT `first instructed here`)
            AND (`services in the ACT` OR `connected with the ACT`)
        `the offer took effect` MEANS `accepted the offer` AND `offer stated it all`
        `the notice took effect` MEANS
                `notified`
            AND (NOT (`the notification is late` `notified` `notified on` `told of the right` `told on`))
        `applies at all` MEANS
                `first instructed here`
            OR  (`s 264 (1) (a), (b) met` AND (`the offer took effect` OR `the notice took effect`))

§§ `ss 269 to 272 and s 274 -- Must my lawyer tell me what it will cost`

DECLARE `Whether the law practice must disclose its costs to me` IS ONE OF
    `yes: in writing and in clear plain language, before or as soon as practicable after it is retained (ss 269, 271, 275), unless a regulation prescribes another exception (s 272 (1) (f), not deposited)`
    `no: the total legal costs are not likely to exceed $1 500, excluding GST and disbursements (s 272 (1) (a), the text's no); if the law practice becomes aware they are likely to, it must disclose as soon as practicable (s 272 (2))`
    `no: you had a disclosure in the previous 12 months, waived further disclosure in writing, and a principal decided on reasonable grounds that it is not justified (s 272 (1) (b), the text's no)`
    `no: you are a client of a kind listed in s 272 (1) (c) (a sophisticated client; the text's no)`
    `no: the costs, or how they are worked out, were agreed after a tender process (s 272 (1) (d), the text's no)`
    `no: you will not be required to pay the costs and they will not otherwise be recovered, as in pro bono work (s 272 (1) (e), the text's no)`

-- s 269 (1): "A law practice must disclose to a client in accordance with this division ..." s 272 (1): "Disclosure ...
-- is not required to be made in any of the following circumstances". s 272 (5) keeps the client's rights to progress
-- reports, to reasonable information, to negotiate a costs agreement and to a bill, whichever answer is given.
@export Must my lawyer give me a written disclosure of the legal costs -- how they are worked out, an estimate, my rights -- under the Legal Profession Act 2006 (ACT) ss 269 to 272?
GIVEN `likely total` IS A NUMBER @desc The total legal costs in the matter you are likely to be charged, in dollars, excluding disbursements (outlays) and GST
      `disclosure in the last 12 months` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you received a costs disclosure from this law practice in the previous 12 months
      `waived in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you agreed in writing to waive your right to a further disclosure
      `principal decided` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a principal of the law practice decided, on reasonable grounds, that a further disclosure is not justified
      `listed in s 272 (1) (c)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a law practice or lawyer, a public, large proprietary or foreign company or its subsidiary, a financial services licensee, a liquidator, administrator or receiver, a large professional partnership, a joint venture of such clients, or a government Minister, department or public authority
      `agreed after a tender` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the legal costs, or how they will be worked out, were agreed after a tender process
      `will not pay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you will not be required to pay the legal costs and they will not otherwise be recovered (for example, pro bono work)
GIVETH A `Whether the law practice must disclose its costs to me`
`s 272 -- must my lawyer disclose the costs to me` `likely total` `disclosure in the last 12 months` `waived in writing` `principal decided` `listed in s 272 (1) (c)` `agreed after a tender` `will not pay` MEANS
    IF `likely total` AT MOST `the threshold amount`
    THEN `no: the total legal costs are not likely to exceed $1 500, excluding GST and disbursements (s 272 (1) (a), the text's no); if the law practice becomes aware they are likely to, it must disclose as soon as practicable (s 272 (2))`
    ELSE IF `disclosure in the last 12 months` AND `waived in writing` AND `principal decided`
         THEN `no: you had a disclosure in the previous 12 months, waived further disclosure in writing, and a principal decided on reasonable grounds that it is not justified (s 272 (1) (b), the text's no)`
         ELSE IF `listed in s 272 (1) (c)`
              THEN `no: you are a client of a kind listed in s 272 (1) (c) (a sophisticated client; the text's no)`
              ELSE IF `agreed after a tender`
                   THEN `no: the costs, or how they are worked out, were agreed after a tender process (s 272 (1) (d), the text's no)`
                   ELSE IF `will not pay`
                        THEN `no: you will not be required to pay the costs and they will not otherwise be recovered, as in pro bono work (s 272 (1) (e), the text's no)`
                        ELSE `yes: in writing and in clear plain language, before or as soon as practicable after it is retained (ss 269, 271, 275), unless a regulation prescribes another exception (s 272 (1) (f), not deposited)`

-- s 274: "(1) If a costs agreement involves an uplift fee, the law practice must disclose to the client in writing,
-- before entering the agreement -- (a) the uplift fee (or the basis for working out the uplift fee); and (b) the reasons
-- why the uplift fee is justified. ... (3) A law practice is not required to make a disclosure under subsection (1) to a
-- sophisticated client."
DECLARE `Whether I must be told about the uplift fee` IS ONE OF
    `yes: before you enter the agreement, in writing, the uplift fee (or how it is worked out) and why it is justified (s 274 (1))`
    `no: you are a sophisticated client (s 274 (3), the text's no)`
    `s 274 does not apply: the agreement has no uplift fee (the text's no: its "if" fails)`

@export Must my lawyer tell me in writing about an uplift fee, and why it is justified, before I sign a no-win no-fee agreement (Legal Profession Act 2006 (ACT) s 274)?
GIVEN `has an uplift fee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the costs agreement provides for an uplift fee (extra legal costs, beyond disbursements, payable if the matter succeeds)
      `listed in s 272 (1) (c)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a law practice or lawyer, a public, large proprietary or foreign company or its subsidiary, a financial services licensee, a liquidator, administrator or receiver, a large professional partnership, a joint venture of such clients, or a government Minister, department or public authority
      `agreed after a tender` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the legal costs, or how they will be worked out, were agreed after a tender process
GIVETH A `Whether I must be told about the uplift fee`
`s 274 -- must I be told about the uplift fee` `has an uplift fee` `listed in s 272 (1) (c)` `agreed after a tender` MEANS
    IF NOT `has an uplift fee`
    THEN `s 274 does not apply: the agreement has no uplift fee (the text's no: its "if" fails)`
    ELSE IF `a sophisticated client` `listed in s 272 (1) (c)` `agreed after a tender`
         THEN `no: you are a sophisticated client (s 274 (3), the text's no)`
         ELSE `yes: before you enter the agreement, in writing, the uplift fee (or how it is worked out) and why it is justified (s 274 (1))`

§§ `s 277 -- If my lawyer did not disclose`
-- A situation: asked only if the law practice failed to disclose something Division 3.2.3 required.

-- s 277: "(1) If a law practice does not disclose ... anything required by this division to be disclosed, the client
-- ... need not pay the legal costs unless they have been assessed under division 3.2.7. (2) [It] may not bring a
-- proceeding against the client ... for the recovery of legal costs unless the costs have been assessed ... (3) ... the
-- client ... may also apply under section 288 ... for the costs agreement to be set aside. (4) ... the amount of the
-- costs may, on an assessment ..., be reduced by an amount considered by the Supreme Court to be proportionate to the
-- seriousness of the failure to disclose."
DECLARE `What follows from the failure to disclose` IS ONE OF
    `s 277 does not apply: the law practice disclosed everything required (the text's no: its "if" fails)`
    `you need not pay the legal costs, and the law practice cannot sue you for them, until they have been assessed (s 277 (1), (2)); on the assessment they may be reduced (s 277 (4)); you may also apply to set aside a costs agreement (s 277 (3))`
    `the costs have been assessed: s 277 (1) and (2) no longer hold them back; the assessed amount may have been reduced for the failure (s 277 (4)); you may also apply to set aside a costs agreement (s 277 (3))`

@export My lawyer did not give me a costs disclosure the Legal Profession Act 2006 (ACT) required: must I pay, and can I be sued, before the costs are assessed (s 277)?
GIVEN `disclosed everything` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the law practice disclosed to you everything Division 3.2.3 required (the costs disclosure, any uplift fee disclosure, any later changes)
      `assessed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the legal costs have been assessed by the Supreme Court under Division 3.2.7
GIVETH A `What follows from the failure to disclose`
`s 277 -- what follows if my lawyer did not disclose` `disclosed everything` `assessed` MEANS
    IF `disclosed everything`
    THEN `s 277 does not apply: the law practice disclosed everything required (the text's no: its "if" fails)`
    ELSE IF `assessed`
         THEN `the costs have been assessed: s 277 (1) and (2) no longer hold them back; the assessed amount may have been reduced for the failure (s 277 (4)); you may also apply to set aside a costs agreement (s 277 (3))`
         ELSE `you need not pay the legal costs, and the law practice cannot sue you for them, until they have been assessed (s 277 (1), (2)); on the assessment they may be reduced (s 277 (4)); you may also apply to set aside a costs agreement (s 277 (3))`

§§ `s 278 -- Progress reports`

-- s 278 (1), (2): on reasonable request, a written report of the progress of the matter, and of the legal costs
-- incurred to date or since the last bill. "A law practice may charge a client a reasonable amount for a report under
-- subsection (1) (a) but must not charge a client for a report under subsection (1) (b)."
DECLARE `Kind of report` IS ONE OF
    `a report of the progress of the matter`
    `a report of the legal costs incurred to date, or since the last bill`

DECLARE `Whether I can be charged for the report` IS ONE OF
    `you are entitled to it on reasonable request, in writing; the law practice may charge a reasonable amount for it (s 278 (1) (a), (2))`
    `you are entitled to it on reasonable request, in writing; the law practice must not charge you for it (s 278 (1) (b), (2), the text's no)`

@export If I ask my lawyer for a written report on my matter or on the costs so far, must they give it and can they charge me (Legal Profession Act 2006 (ACT) s 278)?
GIVEN `the report` IS A `Kind of report` @desc Which report you ask for
GIVETH A `Whether I can be charged for the report`
`s 278 -- can I be charged for a report` `the report` MEANS
    IF `the report` EQUALS `a report of the progress of the matter`
    THEN `you are entitled to it on reasonable request, in writing; the law practice may charge a reasonable amount for it (s 278 (1) (a), (2))`
    ELSE `you are entitled to it on reasonable request, in writing; the law practice must not charge you for it (s 278 (1) (b), (2), the text's no)`

§§ `s 279 -- On what basis can my lawyer charge me`

-- s 279: "legal costs are recoverable -- (a) under a costs agreement made in accordance with division 3.2.5 ...; or (b)
-- if paragraph (a) does not apply -- in accordance with an applicable scale of costs; or (c) if neither ... applies --
-- according to the fair and reasonable value of the legal services provided."
DECLARE `The basis my lawyer can charge on` IS ONE OF
    `under your costs agreement (s 279 (a)), subject to Division 3.2.7 (costs assessment)`
    `in accordance with the applicable scale of costs (s 279 (b))`
    `the fair and reasonable value of the legal services provided (s 279 (c))`

@export On what basis can my lawyer recover legal costs from me -- our costs agreement, a scale of costs, or a fair and reasonable value (Legal Profession Act 2006 (ACT) s 279)?
GIVEN `valid agreement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have a costs agreement with the law practice that was made as Division 3.2.5 requires and is not void (the costs agreement questions check this)
      `scale applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a scale of costs under rules made under the Court Procedures Act 2004 applies to the legal costs
GIVETH A `The basis my lawyer can charge on`
`s 279 -- on what basis can my lawyer charge me` `valid agreement` `scale applies` MEANS
    IF `valid agreement`
    THEN `under your costs agreement (s 279 (a)), subject to Division 3.2.7 (costs assessment)`
    ELSE IF `scale applies`
         THEN `in accordance with the applicable scale of costs (s 279 (b))`
         ELSE `the fair and reasonable value of the legal services provided (s 279 (c))`

§§ `s 282 -- Is my costs agreement made in the form the Act requires`

DECLARE `How the costs agreement was made` IS ONE OF
    `in writing, or evidenced in writing`
    `by a written offer from the law practice that I accepted by conduct`
    `orally, with nothing in writing`

DECLARE `Whether my costs agreement is in the required form` IS ONE OF
    `yes, as far as s 282 goes`
    `no: a costs agreement must be written or evidenced in writing (s 282 (2), the text's no); it is void (s 287 (1))`
    `no: an offer accepted by conduct must clearly state that it is an offer to enter a costs agreement, that it can be accepted in writing or by conduct, and what conduct is acceptance (s 282 (3), (4), the text's no); it is void (s 287 (1))`
    `no: a conditional (no-win no-fee) costs agreement must be signed by you, so acceptance by conduct is not enough (s 283 (3) (c) (iii), the text's no); it is void (s 287 (1))`
    `no: a costs agreement cannot provide that the costs are not subject to costs assessment (s 282 (5), the text's no); it is void (s 287 (1))`

-- s 282: "(2) A costs agreement must be written or evidenced in writing. (3) A costs agreement may consist of a written
-- offer in accordance with subsection (4) that is accepted in writing or by other conduct. (4) The offer must clearly
-- state -- (a) that it is an offer to enter into a costs agreement; and (b) that the offer can be accepted in writing or
-- by other conduct; and (c) the kind of conduct that will be acceptance. (5) Except as provided by section 300A ..., a
-- costs agreement cannot provide that the legal costs to which it relates are not subject to costs assessment". s 304A:
-- a sophisticated client may contract out of Division 3.2.7 (fork F3).
@export Is my costs agreement with my lawyer made in the form the Legal Profession Act 2006 (ACT) requires -- in writing, or by a proper written offer -- and does it keep my right to have the costs assessed (ss 282, 283 (3) (c), 304A)?
GIVEN `how made` IS A `How the costs agreement was made` @desc How the costs agreement was made
      `offer stated it all` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If it was a written offer accepted by conduct: whether the offer clearly stated that it was an offer to enter a costs agreement, that it could be accepted in writing or by other conduct, and the kind of conduct that would be acceptance
      `conditional` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether payment of some or all of the legal costs depends on the matter succeeding (a no-win no-fee or conditional costs agreement)
      `excludes assessment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the agreement says the legal costs are not subject to costs assessment by the Supreme Court
      `listed in s 272 (1) (c)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a law practice or lawyer, a public, large proprietary or foreign company or its subsidiary, a financial services licensee, a liquidator, administrator or receiver, a large professional partnership, a joint venture of such clients, or a government Minister, department or public authority
      `agreed after a tender` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the legal costs, or how they will be worked out, were agreed after a tender process
GIVETH A `Whether my costs agreement is in the required form`
`s 282 -- is my costs agreement in the required form` `how made` `offer stated it all` `conditional` `excludes assessment` `listed in s 272 (1) (c)` `agreed after a tender` MEANS
    IF `how made` EQUALS `orally, with nothing in writing`
    THEN `no: a costs agreement must be written or evidenced in writing (s 282 (2), the text's no); it is void (s 287 (1))`
    ELSE IF `by conduct` AND (NOT `offer stated it all`)
         THEN `no: an offer accepted by conduct must clearly state that it is an offer to enter a costs agreement, that it can be accepted in writing or by conduct, and what conduct is acceptance (s 282 (3), (4), the text's no); it is void (s 287 (1))`
         ELSE IF `by conduct` AND `conditional` AND (NOT `sophisticated`)
              THEN `no: a conditional (no-win no-fee) costs agreement must be signed by you, so acceptance by conduct is not enough (s 283 (3) (c) (iii), the text's no); it is void (s 287 (1))`
              ELSE IF `excludes assessment` AND (NOT `sophisticated`)
                   THEN `no: a costs agreement cannot provide that the costs are not subject to costs assessment (s 282 (5), the text's no); it is void (s 287 (1))`
                   ELSE `yes, as far as s 282 goes`
    WHERE
        `by conduct` MEANS `how made` EQUALS `by a written offer from the law practice that I accepted by conduct`
        `sophisticated` MEANS `a sophisticated client` `listed in s 272 (1) (c)` `agreed after a tender`

§§ `s 283 -- Conditional (no-win no-fee) costs agreements`

DECLARE `What my matter involves` IS ONE OF
    `a criminal proceeding`
    `a proceeding under the Family Law Act 1975 (Cwlth)`
    `neither a criminal proceeding nor a Family Law Act proceeding`

DECLARE `Whether a conditional costs agreement is allowed for my matter` IS ONE OF
    `yes: a conditional costs agreement may relate to your matter (s 283 (2))`
    `no: not in a matter that involves a criminal proceeding (s 283 (2), the text's no)`
    `no: not in a matter that involves a proceeding under the Family Law Act 1975 (Cwlth) (s 283 (2), the text's no)`

-- s 283 (2): "A conditional costs agreement may relate to any matter, except a matter that involves a criminal proceeding
-- or a proceeding under the Family Law Act 1975 (Cwlth)."
@export Can my lawyer and I make a no-win no-fee (conditional costs) agreement for my matter (Legal Profession Act 2006 (ACT) s 283 (2))?
GIVEN `the matter` IS A `What my matter involves` @desc What your matter involves
GIVETH A `Whether a conditional costs agreement is allowed for my matter`
`s 283 (2) -- is a no-win no-fee agreement allowed for my matter` `the matter` MEANS
    IF `the matter` EQUALS `a criminal proceeding`
    THEN `no: not in a matter that involves a criminal proceeding (s 283 (2), the text's no)`
    ELSE IF `the matter` EQUALS `a proceeding under the Family Law Act 1975 (Cwlth)`
         THEN `no: not in a matter that involves a proceeding under the Family Law Act 1975 (Cwlth) (s 283 (2), the text's no)`
         ELSE `yes: a conditional costs agreement may relate to your matter (s 283 (2))`

DECLARE `Whether my conditional costs agreement meets s 283` IS ONE OF
    `yes: it meets s 283 (2) and (3) as they apply to you`
    `no: conditional costs agreements are not allowed in this matter (s 283 (2), the text's no); it is void (s 287 (1))`
    `no: it does not set out what counts as a successful outcome (s 283 (3) (a), the text's no); it is void (s 287 (1))`
    `no: it is not in writing (s 283 (3) (c) (i), the text's no); it is void (s 287 (1))`
    `no: it is not in clear plain language (s 283 (3) (c) (ii), the text's no); it is void (s 287 (1))`
    `no: you did not sign it (s 283 (3) (c) (iii), the text's no); it is void (s 287 (1))`
    `no: it does not state that you were told of your right to seek independent legal advice before entering it (s 283 (3) (d), the text's no); it is void (s 287 (1))`
    `no: it does not give a cooling-off period of at least 5 business days in which you may end it by written notice (s 283 (3) (e), the text's no); it is void (s 287 (1))`

-- s 283 (3): "A conditional costs agreement -- (a) must set out the circumstances that constitute the successful outcome
-- ...; (c) must be -- (i) in writing; and (ii) in clear plain language; and (iii) signed by the client; and (d) must
-- contain a statement that the client has been told of the client's right to seek independent legal advice ...; and (e)
-- must contain a cooling-off period of not less than 5 business days ...". s 283 (5): (c) (iii), (d) and (e) do not
-- apply to a sophisticated client. s 287 (1): a costs agreement that contravenes Division 3.2.5 is void.
@export Does my no-win no-fee (conditional costs) agreement meet the requirements of the Legal Profession Act 2006 (ACT) s 283 -- the successful outcome set out, in writing and plain language, signed by me, the advice statement, and a cooling-off period of at least 5 business days?
GIVEN `the matter` IS A `What my matter involves` @desc What your matter involves
      `outcome set out` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the agreement sets out the circumstances that count as a successful outcome of the matter
      `in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the agreement is in writing
      `plain language` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the agreement is in clear plain language
      `signed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you signed the agreement
      `advice statement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the agreement contains a statement that you were told of your right to seek independent legal advice before entering it
      `cooling-off business days` IS A NUMBER @desc How many business days the agreement's cooling-off period lasts, in which you may end it by written notice (0 if it has none)
      `listed in s 272 (1) (c)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a law practice or lawyer, a public, large proprietary or foreign company or its subsidiary, a financial services licensee, a liquidator, administrator or receiver, a large professional partnership, a joint venture of such clients, or a government Minister, department or public authority
      `agreed after a tender` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the legal costs, or how they will be worked out, were agreed after a tender process
GIVETH A `Whether my conditional costs agreement meets s 283`
`s 283 (3) -- does my no-win no-fee agreement meet the requirements` `the matter` `outcome set out` `in writing` `plain language` `signed` `advice statement` `cooling-off business days` `listed in s 272 (1) (c)` `agreed after a tender` MEANS
    IF NOT (`the matter` EQUALS `neither a criminal proceeding nor a Family Law Act proceeding`)
    THEN `no: conditional costs agreements are not allowed in this matter (s 283 (2), the text's no); it is void (s 287 (1))`
    ELSE IF NOT `outcome set out`
         THEN `no: it does not set out what counts as a successful outcome (s 283 (3) (a), the text's no); it is void (s 287 (1))`
         ELSE IF NOT `in writing`
              THEN `no: it is not in writing (s 283 (3) (c) (i), the text's no); it is void (s 287 (1))`
              ELSE IF NOT `plain language`
                   THEN `no: it is not in clear plain language (s 283 (3) (c) (ii), the text's no); it is void (s 287 (1))`
                   ELSE IF `sophisticated`
                        THEN `yes: it meets s 283 (2) and (3) as they apply to you`
                        ELSE IF NOT `signed`
                             THEN `no: you did not sign it (s 283 (3) (c) (iii), the text's no); it is void (s 287 (1))`
                             ELSE IF NOT `advice statement`
                                  THEN `no: it does not state that you were told of your right to seek independent legal advice before entering it (s 283 (3) (d), the text's no); it is void (s 287 (1))`
                                  ELSE IF `cooling-off business days` LESS THAN 5
                                       THEN `no: it does not give a cooling-off period of at least 5 business days in which you may end it by written notice (s 283 (3) (e), the text's no); it is void (s 287 (1))`
                                       ELSE `yes: it meets s 283 (2) and (3) as they apply to you`
    WHERE
        `sophisticated` MEANS `a sophisticated client` `listed in s 272 (1) (c)` `agreed after a tender`

§§ `s 283 (3) (e) and (6) -- Cooling off`
-- A situation: asked only of a client who has a conditional costs agreement.

DECLARE `Where I stand on cooling off` IS ONE OF
    `you can still end the agreement by written notice, up to and including the last day of its cooling-off period (s 283 (3) (e))`
    `you ended it in time: the law practice may recover only the costs of work done before you ended it, on your instructions and with your knowledge that it would be done in that period, and no uplift fee (s 283 (6))`
    `too late: the cooling-off period has ended (s 283 (3) (e), the text's no)`
    `no cooling-off right under s 283: you are a sophisticated client (s 283 (5), the text's no)`

-- s 283 (3) (e): "a cooling-off period ... during which the client may, by written notice, terminate the agreement".
-- s 283 (6): "If a client terminates a conditional costs agreement within the period ..., the law practice -- (a) may
-- recover only the legal costs in relation to legal services performed for the client before the termination that were
-- performed on the instructions of the client and with the client's knowledge that the legal services would be performed
-- during the period; and (b) ... may not recover the uplift fee (if any)." The period is the agreement's own; its last
-- day is the client's fact (business days are not counted here: NOTES.md).
@export Can I still end my no-win no-fee agreement in its cooling-off period, and if I have, what can my lawyer still charge (Legal Profession Act 2006 (ACT) s 283 (3) (e), (6))?
GIVEN `ended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have ended the agreement by written notice to the law practice
      `ended on` IS A DATE @desc The day you gave that written notice (any date if you have not)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
      `last day` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the cooling-off period the agreement gives you
      `listed in s 272 (1) (c)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a law practice or lawyer, a public, large proprietary or foreign company or its subsidiary, a financial services licensee, a liquidator, administrator or receiver, a large professional partnership, a joint venture of such clients, or a government Minister, department or public authority
      `agreed after a tender` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the legal costs, or how they will be worked out, were agreed after a tender process
GIVETH A `Where I stand on cooling off`
`s 283 (6) -- can I still cool off` `ended` `ended on` `today` `last day` `listed in s 272 (1) (c)` `agreed after a tender` MEANS
    IF `a sophisticated client` `listed in s 272 (1) (c)` `agreed after a tender`
    THEN `no cooling-off right under s 283: you are a sophisticated client (s 283 (5), the text's no)`
    ELSE IF `ended`
         THEN IF `last day` `falls before` `ended on`
              THEN `too late: the cooling-off period has ended (s 283 (3) (e), the text's no)`
              ELSE `you ended it in time: the law practice may recover only the costs of work done before you ended it, on your instructions and with your knowledge that it would be done in that period, and no uplift fee (s 283 (6))`
         ELSE IF `last day` `falls before` `today`
              THEN `too late: the cooling-off period has ended (s 283 (3) (e), the text's no)`
              ELSE `you can still end the agreement by written notice, up to and including the last day of its cooling-off period (s 283 (3) (e))`

§§ `s 284 -- Uplift fees`

DECLARE `Whether the uplift fee is allowed` IS ONE OF
    `allowed by s 284`
    `s 284 does not apply: the agreement provides no uplift fee`
    `not allowed: the agreement is not a conditional costs agreement that meets s 283 (s 284 (1), the text's no); the agreement is void (s 287 (1)), and the uplift fee cannot be recovered and must be repaid if paid (s 287 (4))`
    `not allowed: the basis for working out the uplift fee is not separately identified (s 284 (2), the text's no); the agreement is void (s 287 (1)), and the uplift fee cannot be recovered and must be repaid if paid (s 287 (4))`
    `not allowed: the agreement gives no estimate of the uplift fee, nor a range of estimates with the major variables (s 284 (3), the text's no); the agreement is void (s 287 (1)), and the uplift fee cannot be recovered and must be repaid if paid (s 287 (4))`
    `not allowed: in a litigious matter an uplift fee needs the law practice's reasonable belief that success is reasonably likely (s 284 (4) (a), the text's no); the agreement is void (s 287 (1)), and the uplift fee cannot be recovered and must be repaid if paid (s 287 (4))`
    `not allowed: in a litigious matter the uplift fee is more than 25% of the legal costs otherwise payable, excluding disbursements (s 284 (4) (b), the text's no); the agreement is void (s 287 (1)), and the uplift fee cannot be recovered and must be repaid if paid (s 287 (4))`

-- s 261 "litigious matter": a matter that involves, or is likely to involve, proceedings in a court or tribunal,
-- including any time after proceedings have started or become likely. Worked out from one fact.
-- s 284: "(1) A conditional costs agreement may provide for the payment of an uplift fee. (2) The basis for working out
-- the uplift fee must be separately identified in the agreement. (3) The agreement must contain an estimate of the
-- uplift fee or, if that is not reasonably practicable -- (a) a range of estimates ...; and (b) an explanation of the
-- major variables ... (4) If a conditional costs agreement relates to a litigious matter -- (a) the agreement must not
-- provide for the payment of an uplift fee unless the law practice has a reasonable belief that a successful outcome of
-- the matter is reasonably likely; and (b) the uplift fee must not exceed 25% of the legal costs (excluding
-- disbursements) otherwise payable." Exactly 25% is allowed; anything over is not. An uplift fee outside a conditional
-- costs agreement that meets s 283: fork F2.
@export Is the uplift fee (success fee) in my no-win no-fee agreement allowed by the Legal Profession Act 2006 (ACT) s 284 -- including the 25% cap in court or tribunal matters?
GIVEN `has an uplift fee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the costs agreement provides for an uplift fee (extra legal costs, beyond disbursements, payable if the matter succeeds)
      `valid conditional agreement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the agreement is a conditional (no-win no-fee) costs agreement that meets s 283 (the s 283 question checks this)
      `basis identified` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the agreement separately identifies how the uplift fee is worked out
      `estimate given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the agreement contains an estimate of the uplift fee or, if that was not reasonably practicable, a range of estimates and an explanation of the major variables
      `proceedings started or likely` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether proceedings in a court or tribunal have been started in the matter or are likely
      `reasonable belief` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the law practice had a reasonable belief that a successful outcome of the matter is reasonably likely
      `uplift fee` IS A NUMBER @desc The uplift fee, in dollars
      `costs otherwise payable` IS A NUMBER @desc The legal costs otherwise payable under the agreement, excluding disbursements and the uplift fee, in dollars
GIVETH A `Whether the uplift fee is allowed`
`s 284 -- is my uplift fee allowed` `has an uplift fee` `valid conditional agreement` `basis identified` `estimate given` `proceedings started or likely` `reasonable belief` `uplift fee` `costs otherwise payable` MEANS
    IF NOT `has an uplift fee`
    THEN `s 284 does not apply: the agreement provides no uplift fee`
    ELSE IF NOT `valid conditional agreement`
         THEN `not allowed: the agreement is not a conditional costs agreement that meets s 283 (s 284 (1), the text's no); the agreement is void (s 287 (1)), and the uplift fee cannot be recovered and must be repaid if paid (s 287 (4))`
         ELSE IF NOT `basis identified`
              THEN `not allowed: the basis for working out the uplift fee is not separately identified (s 284 (2), the text's no); the agreement is void (s 287 (1)), and the uplift fee cannot be recovered and must be repaid if paid (s 287 (4))`
              ELSE IF NOT `estimate given`
                   THEN `not allowed: the agreement gives no estimate of the uplift fee, nor a range of estimates with the major variables (s 284 (3), the text's no); the agreement is void (s 287 (1)), and the uplift fee cannot be recovered and must be repaid if paid (s 287 (4))`
                   ELSE IF `proceedings started or likely` AND (NOT `reasonable belief`)
                        THEN `not allowed: in a litigious matter an uplift fee needs the law practice's reasonable belief that success is reasonably likely (s 284 (4) (a), the text's no); the agreement is void (s 287 (1)), and the uplift fee cannot be recovered and must be repaid if paid (s 287 (4))`
                        ELSE IF `proceedings started or likely` AND `uplift fee` GREATER THAN `the most uplift`
                             THEN `not allowed: in a litigious matter the uplift fee is more than 25% of the legal costs otherwise payable, excluding disbursements (s 284 (4) (b), the text's no); the agreement is void (s 287 (1)), and the uplift fee cannot be recovered and must be repaid if paid (s 287 (4))`
                             ELSE `allowed by s 284`
    WHERE
        `the most uplift` MEANS `costs otherwise payable` TIMES `the uplift cap`

-- s 284 (4) (b): the cap, in a litigious matter only. Outside a litigious matter the Act sets no cap (a scope limit with
-- no "only": NOTHING). Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 pt 14.1 may limit costs in personal injury claims (s 284 note; not
-- encoded here).
@export What is the most uplift fee (success fee) my lawyer can charge me under the Legal Profession Act 2006 (ACT) s 284 (4) (b)? In a court or tribunal matter it is 25% of the legal costs otherwise payable, excluding disbursements; otherwise the Act sets no cap (no answer).
GIVEN `proceedings started or likely` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether proceedings in a court or tribunal have been started in the matter or are likely
      `costs otherwise payable` IS A NUMBER @desc The legal costs otherwise payable under the agreement, excluding disbursements and the uplift fee, in dollars
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 284 (4) (b) -- the most uplift fee I can be charged` `proceedings started or likely` `costs otherwise payable` MEANS
    IF `proceedings started or likely`
    THEN JUST (`costs otherwise payable` TIMES `the uplift cap`)
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `s 285 -- Contingency fees`

DECLARE `Whether my agreement is a prohibited contingency fee` IS ONE OF
    `not a contingency fee: s 285 does not prohibit it`
    `allowed: the part worked out from the award or settlement only adopts an applicable scale of costs (s 285 (2))`
    `prohibited: the amount payable is worked out by reference to the award, settlement or property recovered (s 285 (1), the text's no); the agreement is void (s 287 (1)), the law practice can recover nothing for its services in the matter and must repay what you paid for them (s 287 (5))`

-- s 285: "(1) A law practice must not enter into a costs agreement under which the amount payable to the practice, or any
-- part of that amount, is worked out by reference to the amount of any award or settlement or the value of any property
-- that may be recovered in any proceeding to which the agreement relates. (2) Subsection (1) does not apply to the extent
-- that the costs agreement adopts an applicable scale of costs."
@export Is my costs agreement a contingency fee (a percentage of what I win) that the Legal Profession Act 2006 (ACT) s 285 prohibits, and what follows if it is?
GIVEN `worked out from the award` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether any of the amount payable to the law practice is worked out by reference to the amount of an award or settlement, or the value of property recovered, in the proceeding (for example, a percentage of the damages)
      `adopts a scale` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that part only adopts an applicable scale of costs
GIVETH A `Whether my agreement is a prohibited contingency fee`
`s 285 -- is my agreement a prohibited contingency fee` `worked out from the award` `adopts a scale` MEANS
    IF NOT `worked out from the award`
    THEN `not a contingency fee: s 285 does not prohibit it`
    ELSE IF `adopts a scale`
         THEN `allowed: the part worked out from the award or settlement only adopts an applicable scale of costs (s 285 (2))`
         ELSE `prohibited: the amount payable is worked out by reference to the award, settlement or property recovered (s 285 (1), the text's no); the agreement is void (s 287 (1)), the law practice can recover nothing for its services in the matter and must repay what you paid for them (s 287 (5))`

§§ `s 287 -- If my costs agreement is void`
-- A situation: asked only where a costs agreement contravenes Division 3.2.5.

DECLARE `Why my costs agreement is void` IS ONE OF
    `it is not void`
    `it is a prohibited contingency fee agreement (s 285)`
    `it contravenes s 284 (uplift fees)`
    `it contravenes another provision of Division 3.2.5 (for example s 282 or s 283)`

-- s 287: "(2) ... legal costs under a void costs agreement are recoverable as set out in section 279 (b) or (c) [scale,
-- or fair and reasonable value]. (3) However, a law practice is not entitled to recover any amount in excess of the
-- amount that the practice would have been entitled to recover if the costs agreement had not been void and must repay
-- any excess amount received. (4) A law practice that has entered into a costs agreement in contravention of section 284
-- ... is not entitled to recover the uplift fee or any part of it and must repay any amount received in relation to the
-- uplift fee ... (5) A law practice that has entered into a costs agreement in contravention of section 285 ... is not
-- entitled to recover any amount in relation to the provision of legal services in the matter ... and must repay any
-- amount received in relation to the services". Under s 284 the cap is the agreement's amount less the uplift fee
-- (fork F7). Not void: s 287 does not apply (NOTHING).
GIVEN `why void` IS A `Why my costs agreement is void`, `agreement amount` IS A NUMBER, `uplift fee` IS A NUMBER, `fair amount` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`the most recoverable under s 287` `why void` `agreement amount` `uplift fee` `fair amount` MEANS
    CONSIDER `why void`
    WHEN `it is not void` THEN NOTHING
    WHEN `it is a prohibited contingency fee agreement (s 285)` THEN JUST 0
    WHEN `it contravenes s 284 (uplift fees)` THEN JUST (min `fair amount` (`agreement amount` MINUS `uplift fee`))
    WHEN `it contravenes another provision of Division 3.2.5 (for example s 282 or s 283)` THEN JUST (min `fair amount` `agreement amount`)

@export My costs agreement is void under the Legal Profession Act 2006 (ACT) s 287: what is the most my lawyer can recover from me for the work? (No answer if the agreement is not void.)
GIVEN `why void` IS A `Why my costs agreement is void` @desc Why the costs agreement is void, if it is
      `agreement amount` IS A NUMBER @desc What the law practice could have recovered under the agreement had it been valid, including any uplift fee, in dollars
      `uplift fee` IS A NUMBER @desc The uplift fee in that amount, in dollars (0 if none)
      `fair amount` IS A NUMBER @desc The legal costs under the applicable scale of costs or, if none applies, the fair and reasonable value of the work, in dollars (as assessed)
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 287 -- the most my lawyer can recover under a void agreement` `why void` `agreement amount` `uplift fee` `fair amount` MEANS
    `the most recoverable under s 287` `why void` `agreement amount` `uplift fee` `fair amount`

@export My costs agreement is void under the Legal Profession Act 2006 (ACT) s 287: how much must my lawyer repay me? (No answer if the agreement is not void.)
GIVEN `why void` IS A `Why my costs agreement is void` @desc Why the costs agreement is void, if it is
      `agreement amount` IS A NUMBER @desc What the law practice could have recovered under the agreement had it been valid, including any uplift fee, in dollars
      `uplift fee` IS A NUMBER @desc The uplift fee in that amount, in dollars (0 if none)
      `fair amount` IS A NUMBER @desc The legal costs under the applicable scale of costs or, if none applies, the fair and reasonable value of the work, in dollars (as assessed)
      `paid` IS A NUMBER @desc How much you have paid the law practice for its legal services in the matter, in dollars
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 287 -- what my lawyer must repay me` `why void` `agreement amount` `uplift fee` `fair amount` `paid` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the most recoverable under s 287` `why void` `agreement amount` `uplift fee` `fair amount`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHEN JUST `the most` THEN JUST (max 0 (`paid` MINUS `the most`))

§§ `s 288 -- Setting aside a costs agreement`

DECLARE `What s 288 offers me` IS ONE OF
    `you may apply to the Supreme Court; on these facts it may set the agreement, or a provision of it, aside and fix fair and reasonable costs no greater than the agreement allowed (s 288 (1), (5) to (7))`
    `you may apply to the Supreme Court, but on these facts it would not set the agreement aside (s 288 (1))`
    `s 288 does not offer this: you are not a party to a costs agreement with the law practice (NOTHING-kind)`

-- s 288 (1): "On application by a client who is a party to a costs agreement with a law practice, the Supreme Court may
-- order that the agreement or a provision of the agreement be set aside if satisfied that the agreement or provision is
-- not fair or reasonable." s 277 (3) gives the same right where a required disclosure was not made. Whether the court
-- is satisfied is its judgment, asked as a fact. A client who is not a party: a scope limit with no "only" (NOTHING-kind,
-- fork F8).
@export Can I ask the Supreme Court to set aside my costs agreement, or part of it, as not fair or reasonable (Legal Profession Act 2006 (ACT) s 288)?
GIVEN `a party` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a party to a costs agreement with the law practice
      `not fair or reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court would be satisfied that the agreement, or a provision of it, is not fair or reasonable (it may weigh fraud or misrepresentation, a failure to disclose, the parties' conduct, and how the agreement deals with changed circumstances)
GIVETH A `What s 288 offers me`
`s 288 -- can I have my costs agreement set aside` `a party` `not fair or reasonable` MEANS
    IF NOT `a party`
    THEN `s 288 does not offer this: you are not a party to a costs agreement with the law practice (NOTHING-kind)`
    ELSE IF `not fair or reasonable`
         THEN `you may apply to the Supreme Court; on these facts it may set the agreement, or a provision of it, aside and fix fair and reasonable costs no greater than the agreement allowed (s 288 (1), (5) to (7))`
         ELSE `you may apply to the Supreme Court, but on these facts it would not set the agreement aside (s 288 (1))`

-- s 288 (7): "the Supreme Court must not order the payment of an amount in excess of the amount that the law practice
-- would have been entitled to recover if the costs agreement or the provision ... had not been set aside."
@export If the Supreme Court sets aside my costs agreement, what is the most it can order me to pay (Legal Profession Act 2006 (ACT) s 288 (6), (7))?
GIVEN `fair amount` IS A NUMBER @desc The fair and reasonable legal costs for the work, as the court decides them, in dollars
      `agreement amount` IS A NUMBER @desc What the law practice could have recovered under the agreement (or the provision set aside), in dollars
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 288 (7) -- the most I can be ordered to pay after setting aside` `fair amount` `agreement amount` MEANS
    min `fair amount` `agreement amount`

§§ `s 281 -- Interest on an unpaid bill`

DECLARE `Whether the law practice may charge me interest` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the costs have been unpaid for 30 days or longer after the day the bill was given, and the bill states that interest is payable and the rate; interest may run from the day after the bill was given (s 281 (1), (3))`
    `yes: under your costs agreement, as the bill states that interest is payable, the rate, and that it is payable under the agreement (s 281 (2), (3))`
    `not yet: the costs have not yet been unpaid for 30 days after the day the bill was given (s 281 (1))`
    `no: you paid by the 30th day after the day the bill was given (s 281 (1), the text's no)`
    `no: the bill does not state what s 281 (3) requires (the text's no)`

-- s 281: "(1) A law practice may charge interest on unpaid legal costs if the costs are unpaid for 30 days or longer
-- after the day the practice gave a bill ... (2) A law practice may also charge interest ... in accordance with a costs
-- agreement. (3) A law practice must not charge interest ... unless the bill for the costs states -- (a) that interest
-- is payable on unpaid costs; and (b) the rate of interest; and (c) for interest payable in accordance with a costs
-- agreement -- that the interest is payable under the agreement." s 281 (2) is an additional route ("may also"): where
-- the agreement route fails (no interest term, or the bill does not say the interest is payable under the agreement, which
-- s 281 (3) (c) asks only of agreement interest), the s 281 (1) route is still open on (3) (a) and (b) alone.
-- "Unpaid for 30 days or longer after the day": still
-- unpaid after the 30th day after the bill day (the s 281 example: bill 1 May, unpaid on 1 June). Fork F4.
@export May my lawyer charge me interest on an unpaid bill under the Legal Profession Act 2006 (ACT) s 281?
GIVEN `bill given on` IS A DATE @desc The day the law practice gave you the bill (for a lump sum bill, that bill's day, even if you later asked for or were given an itemised bill: s 281 (5))
      `paid` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have paid the bill
      `paid on` IS A DATE @desc The day you paid it (any date if you have not)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
      `bill states interest and rate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the bill states that interest is payable on unpaid costs, and the rate
      `agreement provides interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your costs agreement provides for interest on unpaid legal costs
      `bill says under the agreement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the bill states that the interest is payable under the costs agreement
GIVETH A `Whether the law practice may charge me interest`
`s 281 -- may my lawyer charge me interest` `bill given on` `paid` `paid on` `today` `bill states interest and rate` `agreement provides interest` `bill says under the agreement` MEANS
    IF `agreement provides interest` AND `bill states interest and rate` AND `bill says under the agreement`
    THEN `yes: under your costs agreement, as the bill states that interest is payable, the rate, and that it is payable under the agreement (s 281 (2), (3))`
    ELSE IF `paid` AND `the days unpaid` AT MOST 30
         THEN `no: you paid by the 30th day after the day the bill was given (s 281 (1), the text's no)`
         ELSE IF `the days unpaid` AT MOST 30
              THEN `not yet: the costs have not yet been unpaid for 30 days after the day the bill was given (s 281 (1))`
              ELSE IF NOT `bill states interest and rate`
                   THEN `no: the bill does not state what s 281 (3) requires (the text's no)`
                   ELSE `yes: the costs have been unpaid for 30 days or longer after the day the bill was given, and the bill states that interest is payable and the rate; interest may run from the day after the bill was given (s 281 (1), (3))`
    WHERE
        `the days unpaid` MEANS
            IF `paid`
            THEN `the days from` `bill given on` `to` `paid on`
            ELSE `the days from` `bill given on` `to` `today`

-- s 281 (4): "Interest charged ... must not exceed -- (a) if a rate is prescribed by regulation -- that rate; or (b) if
-- a rate is not prescribed by regulation -- the rate applying under the Court Procedures Rules 2006, schedule 2, part
-- 2.2 (Interest after judgment)." That rate, for any half-year after 30 June 2010, is 6% above the cash rate the Reserve
-- Bank last published before the half-year began (items 2.3 and 2.4, the same for the Supreme and Magistrates Courts).
-- No regulation is deposited (NOTES.md).
@export What is the highest interest rate my lawyer can charge on unpaid legal costs (Legal Profession Act 2006 (ACT) s 281 (4))? It is 6 percentage points above the Reserve Bank cash rate last published before the current half-year began, unless a regulation prescribes a rate.
GIVEN `cash rate` IS A NUMBER @desc The Reserve Bank of Australia cash rate last published before the start of the current half-year (1 January or 1 July), in percent per year (for example 3.6)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 281 (4) -- the highest interest rate on my unpaid costs` `cash rate` MEANS
    `cash rate` PLUS `the margin over the cash rate`

§§ `ss 289 and 292 -- Bills, itemised bills and when I can be sued`

DECLARE `Kind of bill` IS ONE OF
    `a lump sum bill`
    `an itemised bill`

DECLARE `Whether I can ask for an itemised bill` IS ONE OF
    `yes: ask by the last day, 90 days after the day the lump sum bill was given; the law practice must comply as soon as practicable and cannot charge for it (s 292 (3), (4), (8))`
    `you asked in time: the law practice must comply as soon as practicable and cannot charge for it (s 292 (4), (8))`
    `no: more than 90 days have passed since the day the lump sum bill was given (s 292 (3), the text's no)`
    `no: the lump sum bill is for $1 500 or less, excluding GST and disbursements (s 269 (1) (c), s 292 (1), the text's no)`
    `s 292 does not apply: the bill is already itemised (the text's no: its "applies if" fails)`

-- s 292: "(1) This section applies if a lump sum bill is given by a law practice for legal costs exceeding the threshold
-- amount. (2) Any person who is entitled to apply for an assessment ... may ask the law practice for an itemised bill.
-- (3) A request ... must be made not later than 90 days after the day the lump sum bill is given ... (4) The law
-- practice must comply with the request as soon as practicable. ... (8) A law practice is not entitled to charge a
-- person for the preparation of an itemised bill". s 269 (1) (c): the client "is not entitled to request an itemised
-- bill if the bill is for an amount equal to or less than the threshold amount".
@export Can I ask my lawyer for an itemised bill instead of a lump sum bill, and by when (Legal Profession Act 2006 (ACT) s 292)?
GIVEN `kind of bill` IS A `Kind of bill` @desc Which kind of bill you were given
      `bill amount` IS A NUMBER @desc The legal costs in the bill, in dollars, excluding GST and disbursements
      `bill given on` IS A DATE @desc The day the bill was given to you
      `asked` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have asked the law practice for an itemised bill
      `asked on` IS A DATE @desc The day you asked (any date if you have not)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `Whether I can ask for an itemised bill`
`s 292 -- can I ask for an itemised bill` `kind of bill` `bill amount` `bill given on` `asked` `asked on` `today` MEANS
    IF `kind of bill` EQUALS `an itemised bill`
    THEN `s 292 does not apply: the bill is already itemised (the text's no: its "applies if" fails)`
    ELSE IF `bill amount` AT MOST `the threshold amount`
         THEN `no: the lump sum bill is for $1 500 or less, excluding GST and disbursements (s 269 (1) (c), s 292 (1), the text's no)`
         ELSE IF `the last day` `falls before` `the day asked`
              THEN `no: more than 90 days have passed since the day the lump sum bill was given (s 292 (3), the text's no)`
              ELSE IF `asked`
                   THEN `you asked in time: the law practice must comply as soon as practicable and cannot charge for it (s 292 (4), (8))`
                   ELSE `yes: ask by the last day, 90 days after the day the lump sum bill was given; the law practice must comply as soon as practicable and cannot charge for it (s 292 (3), (4), (8))`
    WHERE
        `the last day` MEANS `bill given on` `plus days` 90
        `the day asked` MEANS IF `asked` THEN `asked on` ELSE `today`

@export What is the last day I can ask my lawyer for an itemised bill (Legal Profession Act 2006 (ACT) s 292 (3))? No answer if the bill is itemised already or is for $1 500 or less.
GIVEN `kind of bill` IS A `Kind of bill` @desc Which kind of bill you were given
      `bill amount` IS A NUMBER @desc The legal costs in the bill, in dollars, excluding GST and disbursements
      `bill given on` IS A DATE @desc The day the bill was given to you
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`s 292 (3) -- the last day to ask for an itemised bill` `kind of bill` `bill amount` `bill given on` MEANS
    IF `kind of bill` EQUALS `a lump sum bill` AND `bill amount` GREATER THAN `the threshold amount`
    THEN JUST (`bill given on` `plus days` 90)
    ELSE NOTHING

-- The law practice's bar on suing. s 289 (1): "A law practice must not start a legal proceeding to recover legal costs
-- from a person until at least 30 days after the day the practice has given a bill to the person in accordance with
-- section 290 ... and section 291". s 292 (6): "... until at least 90 days after the day the person is given the [lump
-- sum] bill." s 292 (7): "However, if the person asks for an itemised bill ..., the law practice must not start any
-- proceeding ... until at least 30 days after the day the person is given an itemised bill." "Until at least N days
-- after the day": all N days after the bill day must have passed, so the prohibition covers days 0 to N and the earliest
-- day is day N + 1 after the bill day (fork F5: the clear-days reading of "at least N days").

-- The bars' lengths, in one place, used by both suing questions.
`the s 289 (1) days` MEANS 30
`the s 292 (6) days` MEANS 90
`the s 292 (7) days` MEANS 30

-- The last day (counted from the bill day) on which a bar of "at least N days after the day" forbids a proceeding.
GIVEN `the days after` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the last day barred by a bar of` `the days after` MEANS `the days after`

-- The earliest day a bar of "at least N days after" a given day allows a proceeding: the day after the last day barred.
GIVEN `the days after` IS A NUMBER, `the start` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the first day clear of a bar of` `the days after` `after` `the start` MEANS
    `the start` `plus days` (`the last day barred by a bar of` `the days after` PLUS 1)
DECLARE `Party` IS ONE OF `the client`, `the law practice`
DECLARE `Costs step` IS ONE OF
    `start a proceeding to recover the legal costs`
    `apply for a costs assessment`

GIVEN `the days barred` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party` `Costs step`
`no proceeding to recover the costs within the days barred` `the days barred` MEANS
    PARTY `the law practice`
    SHANT `start a proceeding to recover the legal costs`
    WITHIN `the days barred`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the law practice` BECAUSE "a proceeding to recover legal costs was started before the days set by s 289 (1) or s 292 (6), (7) of the Legal Profession Act 2006 (ACT) had passed"

-- Runs the bar over a proceeding started on day `the day` (counted from the bill day); FULFILLED means the bar was
-- respected. Not exported; takes no DEONTIC from outside.
GIVEN `the days after` IS A NUMBER, `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `starting on this day respects a bar of` `the days after` `the day` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`no proceeding to recover the costs within the days barred` `the days barred`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day counted`), (EVENT `the law practice` `start a proceeding to recover the legal costs` `the day counted`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE
    WHERE
        `the days barred` MEANS `the last day barred by a bar of` `the days after`
        `the day counted` MEANS IF `the day` LESS THAN 0 THEN 0 ELSE `the day`

DECLARE `Whether my lawyer may sue me for the costs on that day` IS ONE OF
    `as far as Part 3.2 goes, yes`
    `no: the law practice is not entitled to recover any legal costs on the claim, because of a claim farming conviction (s 307A (2), the text's no)`
    `no: it has not given you a bill complying with ss 290 and 291 (s 289 (1), the text's no)`
    `no: the costs must first be assessed, because it did not make a disclosure required by Division 3.2.3 (s 277 (2), the text's no)`
    `no: a costs assessment has been applied for and is not completed (s 298 (b), the text's no)`
    `no: not until at least 30 days after the day the bill was given (s 289 (1), the text's no)`
    `no: not until at least 90 days after the day the lump sum bill was given (s 292 (6), the text's no)`
    `no: you asked for an itemised bill and have not been given it (s 292 (7), the text's no)`
    `no: not until at least 30 days after the day the itemised bill was given (s 292 (7), the text's no)`

-- s 298 (b): if an application for a costs assessment is made, "the law practice must not start a proceeding to recover
-- the legal costs until the costs assessment has been completed". s 289 (2): a court may authorise a proceeding sooner
-- if the bill was given and the person is about to leave the ACT; taken to lift the s 289 (1) bar only (fork F6). s 289
-- (4): with or without a costs agreement. s 307A: a claim farming conviction under the Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 part
-- 15.4A leaves no legal costs on that claim recoverable.
@export Can my lawyer start court proceedings against me on a given day to recover legal costs, under the Legal Profession Act 2006 (ACT) ss 289, 292, 277 (2), 298 and 307A?
GIVEN `claim farming conviction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the law practice, or an associate, has been convicted or found guilty of claim farming under the Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 part 15.4A in relation to your claim
      `bill given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the law practice has given you a bill, signed and given as s 290 requires, with the written statement of your rights s 291 requires (that statement is not needed for a sophisticated client)
      `bill given on` IS A DATE @desc The day the bill was given to you (any date if none)
      `kind of bill` IS A `Kind of bill` @desc Which kind of bill you were given
      `bill amount` IS A NUMBER @desc The legal costs in the bill, in dollars, excluding GST and disbursements
      `asked for itemised` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked for an itemised bill within 90 days after the day a lump sum bill was given
      `itemised given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the itemised bill you asked for has been given to you
      `itemised given on` IS A DATE @desc The day the itemised bill was given to you (any date if not)
      `disclosed everything` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the law practice disclosed to you everything Division 3.2.3 required
      `assessed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the legal costs have been assessed by the Supreme Court
      `assessment pending` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an application for a costs assessment has been made and the assessment is not yet completed
      `leave to sue sooner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a court has authorised the law practice to start proceedings sooner because you are about to leave the ACT
      `the day` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the law practice starts (or would start) the proceeding
GIVETH A `Whether my lawyer may sue me for the costs on that day`
`ss 289 and 292 -- may my lawyer sue me for the costs on that day` `claim farming conviction` `bill given` `bill given on` `kind of bill` `bill amount` `asked for itemised` `itemised given` `itemised given on` `disclosed everything` `assessed` `assessment pending` `leave to sue sooner` `the day` MEANS
    IF `claim farming conviction`
    THEN `no: the law practice is not entitled to recover any legal costs on the claim, because of a claim farming conviction (s 307A (2), the text's no)`
    ELSE IF NOT `bill given`
         THEN `no: it has not given you a bill complying with ss 290 and 291 (s 289 (1), the text's no)`
         ELSE IF (NOT `disclosed everything`) AND (NOT `assessed`)
              THEN `no: the costs must first be assessed, because it did not make a disclosure required by Division 3.2.3 (s 277 (2), the text's no)`
              ELSE IF `assessment pending`
                   THEN `no: a costs assessment has been applied for and is not completed (s 298 (b), the text's no)`
                   ELSE IF (NOT `leave to sue sooner`) AND (NOT (`starting on this day respects a bar of` `the s 289 (1) days` `days from the bill`))
                        THEN `no: not until at least 30 days after the day the bill was given (s 289 (1), the text's no)`
                        ELSE IF `s 292 applies` AND `asked for itemised` AND (NOT `itemised given`)
                             THEN `no: you asked for an itemised bill and have not been given it (s 292 (7), the text's no)`
                             ELSE IF `s 292 applies` AND `asked for itemised` AND (NOT (`starting on this day respects a bar of` `the s 292 (7) days` `days from the itemised bill`))
                                  THEN `no: not until at least 30 days after the day the itemised bill was given (s 292 (7), the text's no)`
                                  ELSE IF `s 292 applies` AND (NOT `asked for itemised`) AND (NOT (`starting on this day respects a bar of` `the s 292 (6) days` `days from the bill`))
                                       THEN `no: not until at least 90 days after the day the lump sum bill was given (s 292 (6), the text's no)`
                                       ELSE `as far as Part 3.2 goes, yes`
    WHERE
        `s 292 applies` MEANS `kind of bill` EQUALS `a lump sum bill` AND `bill amount` GREATER THAN `the threshold amount`
        `days from the bill` MEANS `the days from` `bill given on` `to` `the day`
        `days from the itemised bill` MEANS `the days from` `itemised given on` `to` `the day`

@export What is the earliest day my lawyer can start court proceedings against me to recover legal costs (Legal Profession Act 2006 (ACT) ss 289, 292)? No answer while the law practice cannot sue at all (no proper bill, an undisclosed and unassessed bill, an assessment pending, an itemised bill asked for and not given, or claim farming).
GIVEN `claim farming conviction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the law practice, or an associate, has been convicted or found guilty of claim farming under the Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 part 15.4A in relation to your claim
      `bill given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the law practice has given you a bill, signed and given as s 290 requires, with the written statement of your rights s 291 requires (that statement is not needed for a sophisticated client)
      `bill given on` IS A DATE @desc The day the bill was given to you (any date if none)
      `kind of bill` IS A `Kind of bill` @desc Which kind of bill you were given
      `bill amount` IS A NUMBER @desc The legal costs in the bill, in dollars, excluding GST and disbursements
      `asked for itemised` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked for an itemised bill within 90 days after the day a lump sum bill was given
      `itemised given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the itemised bill you asked for has been given to you
      `itemised given on` IS A DATE @desc The day the itemised bill was given to you (any date if not)
      `disclosed everything` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the law practice disclosed to you everything Division 3.2.3 required
      `assessed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the legal costs have been assessed by the Supreme Court
      `assessment pending` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an application for a costs assessment has been made and the assessment is not yet completed
      `leave to sue sooner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a court has authorised the law practice to start proceedings sooner because you are about to leave the ACT
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`ss 289 and 292 -- the earliest day my lawyer can sue me` `claim farming conviction` `bill given` `bill given on` `kind of bill` `bill amount` `asked for itemised` `itemised given` `itemised given on` `disclosed everything` `assessed` `assessment pending` `leave to sue sooner` MEANS
    IF     `claim farming conviction`
        OR (NOT `bill given`)
        OR ((NOT `disclosed everything`) AND (NOT `assessed`))
        OR `assessment pending`
        OR (`s 292 applies` AND `asked for itemised` AND (NOT `itemised given`))
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (`the later of` `the s 289 day` `and` `the s 292 day`)
    WHERE
        `s 292 applies` MEANS `kind of bill` EQUALS `a lump sum bill` AND `bill amount` GREATER THAN `the threshold amount`
        `the s 289 day` MEANS IF `leave to sue sooner` THEN `bill given on` ELSE `the first day clear of a bar of` `the s 289 (1) days` `after` `bill given on`
        `the s 292 day` MEANS
            IF `s 292 applies`
            THEN IF `asked for itemised`
                 THEN `the first day clear of a bar of` `the s 292 (7) days` `after` `itemised given on`
                 ELSE `the first day clear of a bar of` `the s 292 (6) days` `after` `bill given on`
            ELSE `bill given on`

§§ `ss 294A, 304 and 304A -- Am I in time to have the costs assessed`

-- The client's duty with a deadline: s 294A (5): "An application by a client ... for a costs assessment ... must be made
-- not later than 12 months after -- (a) the day the bill was given or the request for payment was made ...; or (b) the
-- day the costs were paid if neither a bill was given nor a request was made."
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party` `Costs step`
`apply for a costs assessment within 12 months` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the client`
    MUST `apply for a costs assessment`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the client` BECAUSE "no application for a costs assessment within 12 months after the bill, request for payment or payment (s 294A (5) of the Legal Profession Act 2006 (ACT))"

GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER, `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `applying on this day is in time` `the days allowed` `the day` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`apply for a costs assessment within 12 months` `the days allowed`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day counted`), (EVENT `the client` `apply for a costs assessment` `the day counted`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE
    WHERE
        `the day counted` MEANS IF `the day` LESS THAN 0 THEN 0 ELSE `the day`

DECLARE `Whether I am in time to apply for a costs assessment` IS ONE OF
    `in time: you applied within 12 months (s 294A (5))`
    `still in time: you have until the last day, 12 months after the day the time began (s 294A (5))`
    `out of time: the Supreme Court may still deal with your application if, having regard to the delay and the reasons for it, it decides that is just and fair (s 294A (6))`
    `out of time, and as a sophisticated client you cannot have a late application dealt with (s 294A (7), the text's no)`
    `no costs assessment: the costs are or were the subject of a consumer dispute under chapter 4 (s 304 (1), the text's no)`
    `no costs assessment: as a sophisticated client you contracted out of Division 3.2.7 (s 304A)`

-- s 304: costs that are or have been the subject of a consumer dispute under chapter 4 must not be assessed, unless the
-- council could not resolve the dispute and told the parties they may apply, or referred it for assessment, or the
-- dispute was withdrawn. s 304A: a sophisticated client may contract out of Division 3.2.7. s 294A (3), (4): an
-- application may be made though the costs are paid, and though they were paid without a bill.
@export Am I in time to apply to the ACT Supreme Court for an assessment of my lawyer's costs (Legal Profession Act 2006 (ACT) s 294A, with ss 304 and 304A)? The application must be made within 12 months after the bill or request for payment (or the payment, if there was neither).
GIVEN `time began on` IS A DATE @desc The day the bill was given or the request for payment was made to you; or, if there was neither, the day you paid the costs
      `applied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have applied for a costs assessment
      `applied on` IS A DATE @desc The day you applied (any date if you have not)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
      `listed in s 272 (1) (c)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a law practice or lawyer, a public, large proprietary or foreign company or its subsidiary, a financial services licensee, a liquidator, administrator or receiver, a large professional partnership, a joint venture of such clients, or a government Minister, department or public authority
      `agreed after a tender` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the legal costs, or how they will be worked out, were agreed after a tender process
      `contracted out` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you agreed with the law practice that Division 3.2.7 (costs assessment) does not apply
      `consumer dispute` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the costs are or were the subject of a consumer dispute under chapter 4 (complaints) of the Act
      `dispute released` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the council could not resolve that dispute and told you that you may apply for assessment, or referred it to the Supreme Court for assessment, or the dispute was withdrawn
GIVETH A `Whether I am in time to apply for a costs assessment`
`s 294A -- am I in time to apply for a costs assessment` `time began on` `applied` `applied on` `today` `listed in s 272 (1) (c)` `agreed after a tender` `contracted out` `consumer dispute` `dispute released` MEANS
    IF `consumer dispute` AND (NOT `dispute released`)
    THEN `no costs assessment: the costs are or were the subject of a consumer dispute under chapter 4 (s 304 (1), the text's no)`
    ELSE IF `sophisticated` AND `contracted out`
         THEN `no costs assessment: as a sophisticated client you contracted out of Division 3.2.7 (s 304A)`
         ELSE IF `applied` AND `applying on this day is in time` `the days allowed` `the days to the application`
              THEN `in time: you applied within 12 months (s 294A (5))`
              ELSE IF (NOT `applied`) AND (NOT (`the last day` `falls before` `today`))
                   THEN `still in time: you have until the last day, 12 months after the day the time began (s 294A (5))`
                   ELSE IF `sophisticated`
                        THEN `out of time, and as a sophisticated client you cannot have a late application dealt with (s 294A (7), the text's no)`
                        ELSE `out of time: the Supreme Court may still deal with your application if, having regard to the delay and the reasons for it, it decides that is just and fair (s 294A (6))`
    WHERE
        `sophisticated` MEANS `a sophisticated client` `listed in s 272 (1) (c)` `agreed after a tender`
        `the last day` MEANS `12 months after` `time began on`
        `the days allowed` MEANS `the days from` `time began on` `to` `the last day`
        `the days to the application` MEANS `the days from` `time began on` `to` `applied on`

@export What is the last day I can apply for an assessment of my lawyer's costs without needing the court's leave (Legal Profession Act 2006 (ACT) s 294A (5))? It is 12 months after the day the bill was given or payment was requested (or the costs were paid, if there was neither).
GIVEN `time began on` IS A DATE @desc The day the bill was given or the request for payment was made to you; or, if there was neither, the day you paid the costs
GIVETH A DATE
`s 294A (5) -- the last day to apply for a costs assessment` `time began on` MEANS
    `12 months after` `time began on`

§§ `ss 300C and 302 -- After the assessment`
-- A situation: asked only once the Supreme Court has assessed the costs.

DECLARE `Who pays the costs of the costs assessment` IS ONE OF
    `the law practice, unless the Supreme Court orders otherwise: the legal costs were reduced by 15% or more (s 302 (2) (a))`
    `the law practice, unless the Supreme Court orders otherwise: it failed to comply with the costs disclosure rules of Division 3.2.3 (s 302 (2) (b))`
    `whoever the Supreme Court orders (s 302 (3))`

-- s 302 (2): "Unless the Supreme Court otherwise orders, the law practice ... must pay the costs of the costs assessment
-- if -- (a) on the assessment the legal costs are reduced by 15% or more; or (b) the court is satisfied that the law
-- practice failed to comply with division 3.2.3". Exactly 15% counts.
@export Who pays the costs of the costs assessment of my lawyer's bill (Legal Profession Act 2006 (ACT) s 302)? Usually the law practice, if the costs were cut by 15% or more or it failed to disclose.
GIVEN `costs claimed` IS A NUMBER @desc The legal costs the law practice claimed (the amount put up for assessment), in dollars
      `costs as assessed` IS A NUMBER @desc The legal costs as the Supreme Court assessed them, in dollars
      `disclosure failure` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court is satisfied that the law practice failed to comply with the costs disclosure rules (Division 3.2.3)
GIVETH A `Who pays the costs of the costs assessment`
`s 302 -- who pays the costs of the assessment` `costs claimed` `costs as assessed` `disclosure failure` MEANS
    IF `the reduction` AT LEAST `the 15% mark`
    THEN `the law practice, unless the Supreme Court orders otherwise: the legal costs were reduced by 15% or more (s 302 (2) (a))`
    ELSE IF `disclosure failure`
         THEN `the law practice, unless the Supreme Court orders otherwise: it failed to comply with the costs disclosure rules of Division 3.2.3 (s 302 (2) (b))`
         ELSE `whoever the Supreme Court orders (s 302 (3))`
    WHERE
        `the reduction` MEANS `costs claimed` MINUS `costs as assessed`
        `the 15% mark` MEANS `costs claimed` TIMES `the assessment reduction`

-- s 300C (2): "If an amount of the legal costs has been paid before the assessment was made, any amount by which the
-- amount paid exceeds the amount assessed may be recovered as a debt in a court of competent jurisdiction."
@export After the Supreme Court assessed my lawyer's costs, how much of what I paid can I recover as a debt (Legal Profession Act 2006 (ACT) s 300C (2))?
GIVEN `paid` IS A NUMBER @desc How much of the legal costs you paid before the assessment, in dollars
      `costs as assessed` IS A NUMBER @desc The legal costs as the Supreme Court assessed them, in dollars
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 300C (2) -- how much I can recover after the assessment` `paid` `costs as assessed` MEANS
    max 0 (`paid` MINUS `costs as assessed`)

-- s 300C (3): "If an amount of the legal costs has not been paid -- (a) the assessment is taken to be a judgment of the
-- Supreme Court for the amount of the unpaid legal costs ...; and (b) the rate of interest payable on the amount is the
-- rate applying under the Court Procedures Rules 2006, schedule 2, part 2.2". The unpaid amount is read as the assessed
-- costs less what was paid.
@export After the Supreme Court assessed my lawyer's costs, for how much is the assessment a judgment against me (Legal Profession Act 2006 (ACT) s 300C (3))? Interest runs at the judgment rate.
GIVEN `paid` IS A NUMBER @desc How much of the legal costs you paid before the assessment, in dollars
      `costs as assessed` IS A NUMBER @desc The legal costs as the Supreme Court assessed them, in dollars
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 300C (3) -- the judgment against me after the assessment` `paid` `costs as assessed` MEANS
    max 0 (`costs as assessed` MINUS `paid`)

§§ `s 307A -- Claim farming`
-- A situation: asked only where the law practice or an associate was convicted of claim farming on the claim.

-- s 307A: if a law practice or an associate is convicted or found guilty of an offence under the Civil Law (Wrongs) Act
-- 2002 part 15.4A, "(2) The law practice is not entitled to recover any legal costs in relation to the claim ... (3) The
-- law practice must repay any legal costs received in relation to the claim to the person from whom it was received."
-- No conviction: s 307A does not apply (NOTHING).
@export If my lawyer or an associate was convicted of claim farming on my claim, how much must my lawyer repay me (Legal Profession Act 2006 (ACT) s 307A)? No answer if there was no conviction.
GIVEN `claim farming conviction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the law practice, or an associate of it, has been convicted or found guilty of claim farming under the Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 part 15.4A in relation to your claim
      `paid` IS A NUMBER @desc How much you paid the law practice in legal costs on the claim, in dollars
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 307A -- what my lawyer must repay me after claim farming` `claim farming conviction` `paid` MEANS
    IF `claim farming conviction`
    THEN JUST `paid`
    ELSE NOTHING
